\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The fretboard coordinate system: strings $s$ (with their open-string pitches in standard tuning) and frets $f$, with fret 0 denoting the open string. The four marked positions --- $(1, 0)$ open, and $(2, 5)$, $(3, 9)$, $(4, 14)$ fretted --- all produce the same pitch E4; choosing among them is a fingering decision with direct consequences for playability.}}{13}{figure.3.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Distribution of the raw GuitarBurst difficulty levels over the 716-piece dataset. Dashed lines mark the boundaries of the 8 ordinal classes produced by equal-frequency binning; alternating shades indicate bin membership. The sparsity of the highest levels (levels 17--20 hold 7, 8, 5, and 3 pieces) is what makes direct 20-level classification untenable (Section~\ref {sec:raw20}).}}{20}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Rhythm alignment, schematically. The PDF-derived score contributes the pitch sequence but no timing (top); the matched MIDI performance contributes onset times (bottom). Needleman--Wunsch alignment pairs the two note sequences, absorbing local insertions such as the ornamental F\#4 as gaps, and each PDF note receives the inter-onset interval of its MIDI counterpart as its duration.}}{22}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The RubricNet architecture. Each descriptor $x_i$ is scored independently by a two-parameter subnetwork $g_i$; the bounded scores are summed into the aggregated difficulty score $S(x)$, and an ordinal head maps $S(x)$ to $K$ cumulative class probabilities. No descriptor interacts with any other before the summation, so $g_i(x_i)$ is exactly descriptor $i$'s contribution to the prediction.}}{27}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces The five triangular membership functions over the normalized descriptor range. Each normalized value belongs to at most two adjacent linguistic terms, with memberships summing to 1.}}{30}{figure.4.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Confusion matrix of RubricNet V3 test predictions (seed 0, pooled over the five test folds; cell counts annotated, rows normalized by true-class size). Errors concentrate within one bin of the diagonal.}}{35}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Descriptor importance under three independent measures: RubricNet influence ranges, Random Forest feature importances, and absolute Spearman correlations with the raw difficulty labels (V3 descriptors, fold 0).}}{39}{figure.5.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Mean per-descriptor subnetwork output by true difficulty class (V3 model, fold 0). Contributions rise steadily with difficulty for the influential descriptors; \texttt {open\textunderscore \allowbreak string\textunderscore \allowbreak ratio} falls, consistent with open strings making music easier to play.}}{40}{figure.5.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Per-descriptor contributions $g_i(x_i)$ for an easy and a hard test piece (V3 model, fold 0), sorted by the Chaconne's contributions. The summed scores $S(x) = -3.06$ and $+5.66$ place the pieces in classes 0 and 6, respectively; every bar is one line of the rubric behind those predictions.}}{42}{figure.5.4}%
